    "result_text": "Build succeeds and tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`, keeping status codes unchanged.\n\nThe codebase already had a `ProblemDetails.build(ex, status, detail)` helper (used by the 403/404/500/validation handlers) that populates exactly those fields, and I confirmed that returning `ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>>` makes OfficeFloor emit the `application/problem+json` content type.\n\nI converted the 8 rejection handlers that were still returning plain JSON maps or empty `Void` bodies to that same pattern:\n\n- **400**: `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler` (preserved its `errors` field as a problem-detail property), `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler`, `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler`\n- **409**: `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`, `DuplicateEmailExceptionHandler`, `DuplicateHouseholdExceptionHandler`, `DuplicateTelephoneExceptionHandler`\n- **429**: `DailyLimitExceptionHandler`\n\n**Keeping structural impact low:** each handler is a single-method class with no other methods (WMC_other = 0) and cyclomatic complexity 1, so each edit is a minimal, self-contained rewrite. I reused the existing `ProblemDetails.build` helper rather than adding new logic or growing any class, and touched no pipeline, YAML, or shared code.\n\nThe full test suite (`AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson`, which asserts `application/problem+json` and `$.status == 400`) is green: **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
